---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: lspkit — One engine. Every surface.
---
{# [WEB-DESIGN] Fieldwork: editorial hierarchy and an engineering plate. #}
<div class="home-shell">
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="signal" aria-hidden="true"></span> The language server toolkit</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">One engine.<br><span>Every surface.</span></h1>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-aside">
      <p>Write your analysis once behind <code>EngineApi</code>. Serve it to your VS Code extension over the Language Server Protocol and to Claude Code, Cursor and Codex as an MCP server. Find the hot path and the memory leak with a profiler built into VS Code.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="prose home-proof" aria-labelledby="proof-heading">
    <h2 id="proof-heading">See it running.</h2>
    <p>SharpLsp and Osprey ship the lspkit profiler in VS Code today: hot lines in the source, a flame graph beside it, and ranked memory leak suspects. <a href="/docs/examples/">See both examples</a>.</p>
    {% video "sharplsp-profiler" %}
    {% shots "sharplsp-heatmap", "osprey-heatmap" %}
  </section>
  <figure class="engineering-plate">
    <img src="/assets/images/engine.svg" width="1440" height="600" alt="An orange engine connects through precise circuit paths to two dark terminal surfaces, representing LSP and MCP.">
    <figcaption><span>FIG. A — One engine, two surfaces.</span><span>LSP / MCP / PROFILER</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <section class="journal-preview" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2 id="journal-heading">From the workbench.</h2><a class="text-link" href="/blog/">All notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    {% for post in collections.posts | reverse %}
    <article class="journal-row">
      <span class="eyebrow">Field note / {{ loop.index }}</span>
      <div><h3><a href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.data.title }} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></h3><p>{{ post.data.excerpt }}</p></div>
      <time datetime="{{ post.date | isoDate }}">{{ post.date | dateFormat }}</time>
    </article>
    {% endfor %}
  </section>
</div>
